From b326e789916700dcbbbeb75d30e95ddc374cbce6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 16:55:36 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(render): use supported native world actor definitions

Ordinary mapped Java entities previously skipped the bundled Bedrock client entity definitions. Resolve the target 1.26.51.1 definition beneath accepted packs and retain independent native animation lifetimes, materials, bone visibility and authoritative actor state. The bundled end crystal uses its authored rotation instead of a camera-facing approximation.

Replace the Java appearance only after every selected surface resolves. Preserve Java fallback for missing queries, equipment, sleeping, riding, unsupported materials and active crystal beams, and retain the original shadow radius. Tests exercise the bundled crystal, camera independence and unsupported overrides.

Verify the target 1.26.51.1 crystal animation at 20, 60 and 144 frames per second across three loops. Each nested bone retains its authored -60-degree-per-second rotation and stable random phase. Preserve the native rate instead of changing it to a Java approximation.

Capture position deltas as frame values before creating the native query
callback. World actor playback scopes outlive the frame and are values in
a weak entity map; capturing the entity in that callback retained the weak
key and every removed actor's playback. The regression checks immutable
frame values and captured references directly, avoiding unreliable GC
assertions. This fixes the same lifecycle error already removed from the
player renderer for world actors and end crystals.

Bind shulker attachment face and interpolated peek amount into the native
authored animation graph. The target 1.26.51.1 bundled definitions read
variable.Shulker.FacingDirection and PeekAmount; omitted values render the
lid closed and the actor facing down.

Production resource-merging and bundled graph tests cover all six faces
and lid motion. Live server rendering remains a separate verification.

Run animation sound and particle events for supported mapped world actors.
Collect immutable cues while sampling the graph, then publish them only
after every selected surface and texture resolves. Preserve the shared
runtime's event clocks and pause gating instead of rejecting the entire
definition when it declares effects.

Resolve locators from the selected geometry and pack generation. Match
the drawn actor's native scale and authored script scale, pose and yaw.
Attached emitters consume current actor queries and bounds, and stop
following after actor removal, fallback, lifetime replacement, resource
replacement or a world change. Retain weak actor references in callbacks.

The target 1.26.51.1 actor/particle runtime in the shared actor patch owns
sound aliases, effect initialization, child effects and timeline semantics.
This world renderer now supplies the same inputs as custom actor playback.
Targeted graph tests cover cue delivery, repeated frames, muted loops and
resumption. Locator tests cover nonuniform script scale and directions.
This does not establish full offscreen simulation or native scene parity.

Supply native vertical_speed as float velocityY times 20, and bind
is_in_lava to the client fluid state. Preserve the shared frame
sitting, saddle and dash-cooldown queries. The bundled camel graph
requires these inputs to select its authored pose. Native registrations
link the scalar callbacks 14220edc0/14220ef60 and flag callbacks
142217bd0/142217870/1422185f0. The complete native callbacks run
across 672 authored contexts, including signed-zero motion and stale
component generations. Java comparisons cover live actor inputs only.

Extract the existing query assembly for direct production-path checks.
Bundled unsaddled camel tests exercise standing and sitting graph
selection. Mounted camera offset timing, native motion/fluid producers
and equipped camel rendering remain separate; preserve equipment
and unsupported-query fallback.

Refresh common actor inputs and native metadata when sampling attached
world-actor particles, even when the body is not submitted again. Keep
the existing actor/resource lifetime guards and locator pose. The shared
runtime patch records the fresh native callback evidence and its limits.

Retry a rejected native world draw when its immutable actor inputs change.
Capability downgrade can remove a required query; restoration must admit
the native draw again without replacing playback, resetting its clocks,
or replaying consumed effects. Keep unchanged unsupported inputs cached.
A live guardian control restores typed target metadata for 566 samples
while the prior renderer remains failed. The shared runtime now tracks
query admission errors per frame, and the world adapter retries against
the changed snapshot while retaining its playback instance.
